## Java Spring Boot & Microservices — Master Annotation Reference (Part 3: #101–150)

---

### 🔷 Category 6: Spring Security & JWT

| # | Annotation | Framework / Module | Core Purpose & When to Use | Code Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 101 | `@EnableWebSecurity` | Spring Security (`org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration`) | Activates Spring Security''s web security support and disables Spring Boot''s default auto-configuration. Place on your `SecurityFilterChain` configuration class. In Spring Security 6+, this is combined with `@Configuration`. | `@Configuration`<br>`@EnableWebSecurity`<br>`public class SecurityConfig {`<br>`  @Bean`<br>`  public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {`<br>`    return http`<br>`      .csrf(AbstractHttpConfigurer::disable)`<br>`      .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS))`<br>`      .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth`<br>`        .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()`<br>`        .anyRequest().authenticated())`<br>`      .addFilterBefore(jwtFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)`<br>`      .build();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 102 | `@EnableMethodSecurity` | Spring Security 6+ | Activates **method-level security** annotations (`@PreAuthorize`, `@PostAuthorize`, `@Secured`). Replaces deprecated `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity`. Set `prePostEnabled = true` (default in 6+). Apply at `@Configuration` class level. | `@Configuration`<br>`@EnableWebSecurity`<br>`@EnableMethodSecurity(`<br>`  prePostEnabled = true,`<br>`  securedEnabled = true,`<br>`  jsr250Enabled = true)`<br>`public class SecurityConfig {`<br>`  // now @PreAuthorize, @Secured, @RolesAllowed work on @Service methods`<br>`}` |
| 103 | `@PreAuthorize` | Spring Security | Evaluates a **SpEL expression before** the method executes. The most powerful method security annotation. Supports role checks, `#param` bindings, `authentication` object, and custom bean calls via `@beanName.method()`. | `@Service`<br>`public class SalaryService {`<br>`  @PreAuthorize("hasRole(''HR_MANAGER'') or`<br>`    (hasRole(''EMPLOYEE'') and #empId == authentication.principal.id)")`<br>`  public SalarySlip getSalarySlip(Long empId, int month) {`<br>`    return salaryRepository.findByEmpIdAndMonth(empId, month);`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority(''SALARY_WRITE'') and #dto.amount <= 500000")`<br>`  public void approveSalaryHike(SalaryHikeDto dto) { ... }`<br>`}` |
| 104 | `@PostAuthorize` | Spring Security | Evaluates a SpEL expression **after** the method executes, using `returnObject`. Use when authorization depends on the **returned data** (e.g., return only if the resource belongs to the caller). Method still runs — use only when the side effect is acceptable. | `@Service`<br>`public class DocumentService {`<br>`  @PostAuthorize("returnObject.ownerId == authentication.principal.id`<br>`    or hasRole(''ADMIN'')")`<br>`  public Document findById(Long id) {`<br>`    // always fetches from DB; access check happens on return`<br>`    return documentRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 105 | `@Secured` | Spring Security | Simpler alternative to `@PreAuthorize` — restricts access to **specific roles**. No SpEL support. Use when access control is purely role-based and doesn''t require parameter evaluation. Requires `securedEnabled = true` in `@EnableMethodSecurity`. | `@Service`<br>`public class AdminService {`<br>`  @Secured({"ROLE_ADMIN", "ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN"})`<br>`  public void deleteUser(Long userId) {`<br>`    userRepository.deleteById(userId);`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @Secured("ROLE_ADMIN")`<br>`  public List<AuditLog> getAuditLogs() {`<br>`    return auditRepository.findAll();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 106 | `@RolesAllowed` | Jakarta EE (`jakarta.annotation.security`) / Spring Security | JSR-250 standard annotation for role-based access control. Functionally similar to `@Secured`. Use in environments targeting Jakarta EE portability. Requires `jsr250Enabled = true` in `@EnableMethodSecurity`. | `@RestController`<br>`@RequestMapping("/api/reports")`<br>`public class ReportController {`<br>`  @GetMapping("/financial")`<br>`  @RolesAllowed({"ROLE_FINANCE", "ROLE_ADMIN"})`<br>`  public ResponseEntity<FinancialReport> getFinancialReport() {`<br>`    return ResponseEntity.ok(reportService.getFinancialReport());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 107 | `@AuthenticationPrincipal` | Spring Security | Injects the **currently authenticated principal** (typically a `UserDetails` implementation) directly into a controller method. Cleaner than calling `SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication()` manually. | `@RestController`<br>`@RequestMapping("/api/profile")`<br>`public class ProfileController {`<br>`  @GetMapping`<br>`  public ResponseEntity<ProfileDto> getMyProfile(`<br>`    @AuthenticationPrincipal UserPrincipal currentUser) {`<br>`    return ResponseEntity.ok(`<br>`      profileService.getProfile(currentUser.getId()));`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @PutMapping("/avatar")`<br>`  public void uploadAvatar(`<br>`    @AuthenticationPrincipal UserPrincipal user,`<br>`    @RequestParam MultipartFile file) {`<br>`    storageService.saveAvatar(user.getId(), file);`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 108 | `@WithMockUser` | Spring Security Test | Runs a test with a **mock authenticated user** in the `SecurityContext`. Eliminates the need for actual JWT tokens in `@WebMvcTest` or `@SpringBootTest` tests. Supports `username`, `roles`, `authorities`, `password`. | `@WebMvcTest(AdminController.class)`<br>`class AdminControllerTest {`<br>`  @Autowired private MockMvc mockMvc;`<br>`  @MockBean private AdminService adminService;`<br><br>`  @Test`<br>`  @WithMockUser(username = "admin", roles = {"ADMIN"})`<br>`  void shouldAllowAdminAccess() throws Exception {`<br>`    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/admin/users"))`<br>`      .andExpect(status().isOk());`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @Test`<br>`  @WithMockUser(roles = {"USER"})`<br>`  void shouldDenyNonAdminAccess() throws Exception {`<br>`    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/admin/users"))`<br>`      .andExpect(status().isForbidden());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 109 | `@PermitAll` | Jakarta EE / Spring Security | Marks a method as **accessible without authentication** — bypasses all security checks. Requires `jsr250Enabled = true`. Use on public endpoints like health checks, login, and registration. Prefer `http.requestMatchers(...).permitAll()` for URL-level config. | `@RestController`<br>`@RequestMapping("/api/auth")`<br>`public class AuthController {`<br>`  @PostMapping("/login")`<br>`  @PermitAll`<br>`  public ResponseEntity<AuthResponse> login(`<br>`    @Valid @RequestBody LoginRequest request) {`<br>`    return ResponseEntity.ok(authService.login(request));`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @PostMapping("/register")`<br>`  @PermitAll`<br>`  public ResponseEntity<Void> register(`<br>`    @Valid @RequestBody RegisterRequest request) {`<br>`    authService.register(request);`<br>`    return ResponseEntity.status(201).build();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 110 | `@DenyAll` | Jakarta EE / Spring Security | Unconditionally **denies access** to a method, regardless of the caller''s roles. Use to temporarily disable an endpoint in production without removing its code, or to explicitly block inherited public access on a specific overridden method. | `@Service`<br>`public class BetaFeatureService {`<br>`  @DenyAll // disabled in prod — not ready for release`<br>`  public AnalyticsReport getExperimentalReport() {`<br>`    return analyticsEngine.runExperimentalQuery();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |

