### 🔷 Category 7: Spring Kafka & Async Messaging

| # | Annotation | Framework / Module | Core Purpose & When to Use | Code Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 111 | `@KafkaListener` | Spring Kafka (`org.springframework.kafka.annotation`) | Marks a method as a **Kafka message consumer**. Binds to one or more topics, supports consumer group, concurrency, and container factory. The workhorse annotation for event-driven microservices. | `@Service`<br>`@Slf4j`<br>`public class OrderEventConsumer {`<br>`  @KafkaListener(`<br>`    topics = "${app.kafka.topics.order-placed}",`<br>`    groupId = "inventory-service-group",`<br>`    concurrency = "3",`<br>`    containerFactory = "orderKafkaListenerContainerFactory")`<br>`  public void handleOrderPlaced(`<br>`    @Payload OrderPlacedEvent event,`<br>`    @Header(KafkaHeaders.RECEIVED_TOPIC) String topic,`<br>`    @Header(KafkaHeaders.OFFSET) long offset) {`<br>`    log.info("Received from topic={} offset={}", topic, offset);`<br>`    inventoryService.reserve(event.getItems());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 112 | `@KafkaHandler` | Spring Kafka | Used inside a `@KafkaListener`-annotated **class** (not method) to route messages to different handlers based on the **payload type**. Enables polymorphic message handling in a single listener class. | `@Component`<br>`@KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "order-group")`<br>`public class OrderEventRouter {`<br>`  @KafkaHandler`<br>`  public void handleOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {`<br>`    fulfillmentService.process(event);`<br>`  }`<br>`  @KafkaHandler`<br>`  public void handleOrderCancelled(OrderCancelledEvent event) {`<br>`    inventoryService.release(event.getItems());`<br>`  }`<br>`  @KafkaHandler(isDefault = true)`<br>`  public void handleUnknown(Object payload) {`<br>`    log.warn("Unknown event type: {}", payload.getClass());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 113 | `@EnableKafka` | Spring Kafka | Enables detection of `@KafkaListener` annotations on Spring beans. Auto-configured in Spring Boot when `spring-kafka` is on the classpath. Add explicitly in non-Boot configurations or custom Kafka config classes. | `@Configuration`<br>`@EnableKafka`<br>`public class KafkaConsumerConfig {`<br>`  @Bean`<br>`  public ConsumerFactory<String, Object> consumerFactory() {`<br>`    Map<String, Object> props = new HashMap<>();`<br>`    props.put(BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, bootstrapServers);`<br>`    props.put(GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "order-service");`<br>`    props.put(VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, JsonDeserializer.class);`<br>`    return new DefaultKafkaConsumerFactory<>(props);`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 114 | `@Payload` | Spring Messaging (`org.springframework.messaging.handler.annotation`) | Binds a **method parameter to the message payload** in `@KafkaListener`, `@RabbitListener`, and `@MessageMapping` methods. Supports JSR-303 validation when combined with `@Valid`. | `@KafkaListener(topics = "payment-events")`<br>`public void processPayment(`<br>`  @Payload @Valid PaymentEvent event,`<br>`  Acknowledgment ack) {`<br>`  paymentService.process(event);`<br>`  ack.acknowledge(); // manual commit after success`<br>`}` |
| 115 | `@Header` | Spring Messaging | Binds a **message header value** to a method parameter in `@KafkaListener`, `@RabbitListener`, or `@MessageMapping`. Use to extract Kafka metadata like topic, partition, offset, timestamp, or custom correlation headers. | `@KafkaListener(topics = "audit-events")`<br>`public void onAuditEvent(`<br>`  @Payload AuditEvent event,`<br>`  @Header(KafkaHeaders.RECEIVED_PARTITION) int partition,`<br>`  @Header(KafkaHeaders.OFFSET) long offset,`<br>`  @Header("X-Correlation-ID") String correlationId) {`<br>`  MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);`<br>`  auditService.record(event, partition, offset);`<br>`}` |

---

### 🔷 Category 8: Spring Async, Scheduling & Events

| # | Annotation | Framework / Module | Core Purpose & When to Use | Code Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 116 | `@Async` | Spring (`org.springframework.scheduling.annotation`) | Executes a method **in a separate thread** from a managed `TaskExecutor`. Returns `CompletableFuture<T>`, `Future<T>`, or `void`. Requires `@EnableAsync` on a configuration class. **Important**: caller and callee must be in different Spring beans — self-invocation bypasses the proxy. | `@Service`<br>`public class ReportService {`<br>`  @Async("reportTaskExecutor")`<br>`  public CompletableFuture<ReportDto> generateHeavyReport(Long reportId) {`<br>`    log.info("Generating on thread: {}", Thread.currentThread().getName());`<br>`    ReportDto report = reportEngine.generate(reportId); // long-running`<br>`    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(report);`<br>`  }`<br>`}`<br><br>`// Controller:`<br>`CompletableFuture<ReportDto> future = reportService.generateHeavyReport(id);`<br>`future.thenAccept(r -> notificationService.notify(r));` |
| 117 | `@EnableAsync` | Spring | Enables Spring''s async method execution capability. Required before `@Async` annotations are processed. Define a custom `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` bean named `"taskExecutor"` to control pool size, queue capacity, and thread naming. | `@Configuration`<br>`@EnableAsync`<br>`public class AsyncConfig {`<br>`  @Bean(name = "reportTaskExecutor")`<br>`  public Executor reportTaskExecutor() {`<br>`    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor exec = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();`<br>`    exec.setCorePoolSize(5);`<br>`    exec.setMaxPoolSize(20);`<br>`    exec.setQueueCapacity(100);`<br>`    exec.setThreadNamePrefix("report-async-");`<br>`    exec.initialize();`<br>`    return exec;`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 118 | `@Scheduled` | Spring | Triggers a method on a **fixed schedule**. Supports `cron` (full cron expression), `fixedRate` (ms between start times), `fixedDelay` (ms after last completion), and `initialDelay`. Runs in a single-threaded scheduler by default — use `@Async` together for parallel execution. | `@Component`<br>`@Slf4j`<br>`public class ScheduledJobs {`<br>`  @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 1 * * *") // 1 AM daily`<br>`  public void cleanExpiredTokens() {`<br>`    int deleted = tokenRepository.deleteExpiredBefore(Instant.now());`<br>`    log.info("Cleaned {} expired tokens", deleted);`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @Scheduled(fixedDelayString = "${app.health-check.interval-ms:30000}",`<br>`    initialDelay = 10000)`<br>`  public void checkDownstreamHealth() {`<br>`    healthCheckService.pingAll();`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 119 | `@EventListener` | Spring | Subscribes a method to **Spring Application Events**. Fires synchronously in the same thread by default. Use `@Async` together for non-blocking event handling. Supports SpEL condition filtering via `condition` attribute. Replaces the older `ApplicationListener` interface. | `@Component`<br>`@Slf4j`<br>`public class UserEventHandler {`<br>`  @EventListener`<br>`  @Async`<br>`  public void onUserRegistered(UserRegisteredEvent event) {`<br>`    emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(event.getUserEmail());`<br>`    analyticsService.trackRegistration(event.getUserId());`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @EventListener(condition = "#event.priority == ''HIGH''")`<br>`  public void onHighPriorityAlert(SystemAlertEvent event) {`<br>`    pagerDutyClient.trigger(event.getMessage());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 120 | `@TransactionalEventListener` | Spring | Like `@EventListener` but bound to the **transaction lifecycle phase** of the publishing transaction. Use `AFTER_COMMIT` (default) to guarantee the event handler runs only after the DB transaction commits — critical for Kafka producers, external API calls, and email triggers that must not fire on rollback. | `@Component`<br>`public class OrderEventRelay {`<br>`  @TransactionalEventListener(`<br>`    phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)`<br>`  public void publishToKafka(OrderConfirmedEvent event) {`<br>`    // Only fires if the DB transaction that raised this event committed`<br>`    kafkaTemplate.send("order-confirmed", event.getOrderId().toString(),`<br>`      event);`<br>`  }`<br><br>`  @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_ROLLBACK)`<br>`  public void alertOnRollback(OrderConfirmedEvent event) {`<br>`    log.error("Order {} transaction rolled back!", event.getOrderId());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |

---

### 🔷 Category 9: Resilience4j — Advanced Patterns

| # | Annotation | Framework / Module | Core Purpose & When to Use | Code Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 121 | `@Bulkhead` | Resilience4j Spring Boot (`io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead`) | Limits **concurrent calls** to a method (thread-pool or semaphore isolation). Prevents one slow downstream service from exhausting all threads. Use `type = THREADPOOL` for async and `SEMAPHORE` for reactive/sync scenarios. | `@Service`<br>`public class ExternalReportService {`<br>`  @Bulkhead(`<br>`    name = "reportBulkhead",`<br>`    type = Bulkhead.Type.THREADPOOL,`<br>`    fallbackMethod = "reportFallback")`<br>`  public CompletableFuture<Report> generateReport(Long id) {`<br>`    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(()-> heavyReportEngine.run(id));`<br>`  }`<br>`  private CompletableFuture<Report> reportFallback(Long id, BulkheadFullException e) {`<br>`    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(Report.queued(id));`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |
| 122 | `@TimeLimiter` | Resilience4j Spring Boot | Sets a **maximum execution time** for async calls (used with `CompletableFuture`). Throws `TimeoutException` if the configured `timeoutDuration` is exceeded. Always pair with `@CircuitBreaker` for complete fault tolerance. | `@Service`<br>`public class GeocodingService {`<br>`  @TimeLimiter(name = "geoApi", fallbackMethod = "geoFallback")`<br>`  @CircuitBreaker(name = "geoApi")`<br>`  public CompletableFuture<GeoLocation> locate(String address) {`<br>`    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(`<br>`      () -> externalGeoClient.geocode(address));`<br>`  }`<br>`  private CompletableFuture<GeoLocation> geoFallback(`<br>`    String address, TimeoutException ex) {`<br>`    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(GeoLocation.unknown());`<br>`  }`<br>`}` |

